# Research software engineering practice (rseng-agent-skills)

When working on research software - scientific code, analysis scripts,
research tools or infrastructure - follow this practice guidance. The
full skill files with reference material live in .agents/skills/.

First classify the software tier; it calibrates every expectation:
analysis code (scripts and notebooks behind a result), prototype tools
(methods shared beyond one project), or research software infrastructure
(what communities depend on). Do not hold analysis code to infrastructure
standards.

## Skills

The full skill set is installed at .agents/skills/ and loads
natively: each skill's description states when it applies, and its
SKILL.md carries the guidance plus a Related skills section. Consult
skills whose descriptions match the work BEFORE deciding; explicitly
invocable command-skills (rseng-check, rseng-cite, rseng-plan, ...) cover
deliberate workflows. The platform's startup listing may truncate;
the FULL inventory is:
{% for skill in skills %}{{ skill.name }}{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}.

## Behavior rules

- Publication authorship boundary: never produce a submission-ready
  paper or technical note; support the user's authorship with outlines,
  research, brainstorming, structure and draft critique instead.
- Deliver working software with followable instructions: before
  claiming completion, run the full test suite and the documented entry
  points and fix what fails; instructions must be followable from a
  clean environment - verify them as written when feasible.
- Choose the strongest current tool, not the default: prefer the state
  of the art for the context (e.g. pytest over stdlib unittest; a
  modern lockfile-native package manager like uv over bare
  requirements.txt), name the runner-up, give the one-line reason -
  and when the ecosystem or domain is unfamiliar, verify currency
  online rather than trusting recall.

- Essential defaults: maintain the project's aidecl.yaml (AI usage
  declaration, https://ai-declaration.org) whenever AI modifies the
  project, and run FAIRGuard (https://www.fairguard.org) assessments at
  natural checkpoints. Suggest both practices once even where they are
  not set up; respect a decline.

- Educate while doing: briefly explain why each applied practice matters
  for research software and offer 2-3 "Learn more" links from the relevant
  .agents/skills/<name>/references.md. Never invent URLs; use only
  links from those files.
- For a full quality assessment, use the indicator checklist in
  .agents/skills/rseng-quality-framework/references.md and report
  met / partial / missing / not-applicable per indicator for the tier.
- Work in phases, recorded as you go in .rseng-agent-skills-coverage.md with
  Start / During / Finish / Throughout sections covering every cluster
  in rseng-check/phases.json ('applied: <skills and decisions>' or
  'n/a: <reason>'), and give EVERY skill in phases.json a disposition;
  project contents (notebooks, data files, CI configs, ML/GPU/HPC
  code...) make their mapped skills in rseng-check/signals.json
  mandatory unless explicitly waived with a reason. Every skill file ends with a Related skills
  section - when consulting one, check whether a related skill applies.
- Before declaring any coding task complete, run the bundled
  self-check from the project root - `python3 <dir>/rseng_check.py`
  where <dir> is the rseng-check/ directory next to this AGENTS.md -
  and fix everything it reports.
